# Copy to .env and adjust. Never commit the real .env.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.db.yml up -d
#
# Once .env exists, do not copy over it again — it holds real secrets by then. To pick up
# settings added to this file since, without disturbing anything already set:
#
#   just env-sync check   # report what would change
#   just env-sync         # append the new keys, keeping every value you have set

# --- Database credentials (used by the postgres image on first init) ----------
# These are read only when the data volume is empty. Changing them later does
# not change the role or database that already exists in the volume.
POSTGRES_USER=conjectures
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me-strong-password
POSTGRES_DB=conjectures
# Host port only; inside compose the database is always db:5432. Change this if
# another Postgres already holds 5432 on your machine.
POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# --- Read-only monitoring role created by deploy/db/00_init.sh ----------------
MONITOR_PASSWORD=change-me-monitor-password

# --- Connecting from the host (outside compose) -------------------------------
# Inside compose, services reach the database as `db`. Set this only to run
# scripts or tests from your machine against the container:
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg://conjectures:change-me-strong-password@localhost:5432/conjectures

# --- Submission payments and dynamic bounties --------------------------------
# REQUIRED. The coldkey that receives each exact 0.5-TAO submission payment.
# PAYMENT_RECIPIENT_SS58=5C4h...
# PAYMENT_AMOUNT_RAO=500000000

# The Subnet 66 Alpha stake position whose finalized balance is B in
# b_i = c * B * N * w_i / W. The coldkey defaults to the payment recipient; production requires
# the hotkey explicitly so the validator cannot price from the wrong stake position.
# BOUNTY_WALLET_COLDKEY_SS58=5C4h...
# BOUNTY_WALLET_HOTKEY_SS58=5F3s...
# BOUNTY_NETUID=66

# Dynamic bounty policy. The default constant is 1/4 and age weight increments once per day.
# BOUNTY_POLICY_VERSION=dynamic-age-v1
# BOUNTY_CONSTANT_NUMERATOR=1
# BOUNTY_CONSTANT_DENOMINATOR=4
# BOUNTY_AGE_PERIOD_SECONDS=86400
# BOUNTY_BALANCE_CACHE_SECONDS=60
# BITTENSOR_NETWORK=finney

# Optional live USD display value for each bounty. TaoStats combines the Subnet 66 Alpha/TAO
# pool price with TAO/USD; without a key (or during an upstream outage), amount_usd is null and
# the Alpha amount remains available. Create a key at https://taostats.io/pro/.
# TAOSTATS_API_KEY=
# TAOSTATS_PRICE_CACHE_SECONDS=60

# --- Treasury emissions ------------------------------------------------------
# The emissions worker waits for each observed Subnet 66 epoch, then submits one weight:
# treasury UID 121 receives 100%. Netuid and UID are intentional code constants, so changing
# either requires a reviewed code change rather than an environment edit on a live validator.
#
# Only the named wallet directory is mounted into the worker. Its hotkey must be registered on
# Subnet 66 and able to set weights; the SDK signs with that hotkey and supports the subnet's
# plaintext or commit-reveal mode automatically.
# EMISSIONS_WALLET_HOST_PATH=/home/validator/.bittensor/wallets
# EMISSIONS_WALLET_NAME=validator
# EMISSIONS_WALLET_HOTKEY=default
# EMISSIONS_NETWORK=finney
# EMISSIONS_RETRY_SECONDS=30

# Development-only deterministic Alpha balance. Production refuses this override and reads the
# configured stake position on finalized chain state.
# BOUNTY_POOL_BALANCE_RAO=4000000000

# --- External task repository ------------------------------------------------
# Task bundles, pool metadata, fixtures, and task-fetch/build tooling live in a
# separate checkout. The sibling path is the default for host processes; set
# this only when the repositories use a different layout.
# CONJECTURES_TASKS_ROOT=../conjectures-tasks

# --- Public read surface ------------------------------------------------------
# The catalog, results, and system endpoints are unauthenticated and read by a
# browser. Everything below has a working development default; the four marked
# REQUIRED IN PROD are refused at startup if left unset with APP_MODE=PROD.

# Exact browser origins allowed to read /v1, comma-separated. No wildcard and no
# pattern: scheme://host[:port], no trailing slash, https in production. Empty is
# valid and fail-closed — it means no browser may read the API.
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://conjectures.io,https://www.conjectures.io

# Exact browser origins allowed to make a *state-changing* request to /v1. Same
# format and the same production rules as CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS, and it defaults to
# that list when unset — most deployments have one website and want one list.
#
# Set it to narrow the grant: an origin that may read the public catalog is not
# thereby an origin that may spend an account's credits or repoint its payout.
# Setting it to the empty string is meaningful and different from leaving it unset
# — it means no browser may write to this API at all, which is the right answer for
# a deployment that serves only miner tooling.
#
# This is what a signed-in browser's writes are checked against: a write on a
# cookie session must arrive with an Origin on this list, or with Sec-Fetch-Site
# same-origin. Neither header can be set by a page, which is what makes them proof.
# WRITE_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://conjectures.io

# REQUIRED IN PROD. Signs the opaque page cursors on the result feeds. Any value
# of 32+ characters; the published development constant is refused in production.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# PUBLIC_CURSOR_SECRET=

# REQUIRED IN PROD. Salts the per-conjecture solver pseudonyms on
# /v1/catalog/conjectures/{slug}/activity. A hotkey is a 48-character address
# from a known alphabet, so an unsalted or publicly-salted digest is reversible
# by enumeration. Rotating this renames every pseudonym, which is intended.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# PUBLIC_ACTIVITY_SALT=

# Per-client request ceiling on /v1. The limiter is in-process, so N replicas
# admit N times this rate: set the per-replica share, not the global one. Cannot
# be disabled in production.
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=true
# RATE_LIMIT_REQUESTS=120
# RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=60
# RATE_LIMIT_MAX_CLIENTS=50000

# How many rightmost X-Forwarded-For entries this deployment's own proxies added.
# 0 (the default) ignores the header entirely and uses the peer address, which is
# correct for a directly exposed process and is the only setting that cannot be
# spoofed. Set it to the real hop count behind a CDN or load balancer, or every
# visitor collapses onto one rate-limit budget.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=0

# Cache-Control max-age on the public reads. 0 sends no-store.
# PUBLIC_CACHE_SECONDS=60

# Advertises an HTTP/3 authority for clients to retry over QUIC. Defaults to
# h3=":443"; ma=86400 in production and to nothing outside it. The ASGI app never
# sees the transport; this describes the deployment's edge, so change it if the
# edge does not terminate QUIC on 443.
# ALT_SVC=h3=":443"; ma=86400

# HSTS max-age, sent in production only. 0 disables the header.
# HSTS_MAX_AGE=31536000

# --- Operating switches -------------------------------------------------------
# Refuses POST /v1/submissions with 503 SUBMISSIONS_PAUSED and reports
# submissions_open: false on /v1/system/status. Set during the weekly pin
# rotation; the drain cannot complete while new work keeps arriving.
# SUBMISSIONS_PAUSED=false

# Operator message shown on /v1/system/status. Max 500 characters.
# STATUS_BANNER=

# The weekly drain-and-rotate window, reported on /v1/system/status so a solver
# can see when submissions are expected to close. Monday is 0, Sunday is 6.
# PIN_ROTATION_WEEKDAY=1
# PIN_ROTATION_START_UTC=02:00
# PIN_ROTATION_DURATION_MINUTES=240

# --- Accounts and sessions (Stage 2) ------------------------------------------
# The signed-in surface: /v1/auth, /v1/me, and the credit-funded submission path.
# See docs/ACCOUNT_API.md.

# REQUIRED IN PROD. Where the emailed sign-in link points. The WEBSITE's origin,
# not this API's: the link is clicked by a person in a browser and lands on a
# page, which then calls the API. Must be https in production.
# WEBSITE_BASE_URL=https://conjectures.io

# REQUIRED IN PROD (as `smtp`). `console` writes sign-in links — which are
# credentials — to the process log, so production refuses it. SMTP failures return
# a 503 and never log the recipient or link. See submission_api/mail.py.
# MAIL_SENDER=smtp

# REQUIRED when MAIL_SENDER=smtp. Works with any SMTP provider or relay. The
# default is STARTTLS on port 587; use `implicit-tls` with port 465. Production
# refuses `none`. Username/password may both be omitted only for an authenticated
# network relay. Publish the SPF/DKIM records supplied by the chosen provider,
# then add a DMARC policy for the From domain.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USERNAME=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# SMTP_FROM_ADDRESS=login@conjectures.io
# SMTP_SECURITY=starttls
# SMTP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10

# Goes into every signed login message, binding the signature to this deployment
# so one produced for staging or a third-party instance is not valid here.
# LOGIN_DOMAIN=conjectures.io

# Public OAuth web-client identifier for Google Identity Services. Empty disables
# Google sign-in without affecting wallet or magic-link login. This is not a
# secret; ID tokens are accepted only when their signed audience matches it.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=1081377001123-4kkh4sfuemmr66b5a5sl64b8d6jlhmme.apps.googleusercontent.com

# Session lifetime. Rolling: extended on use, so an active session does not
# expire under someone. The refresh interval is what keeps that from meaning a
# database write on every authenticated request.
# SESSION_DAYS=30
# SESSION_REFRESH_MINUTES=60

# Single-use credentials in transit, so both are short. Fifteen minutes is long
# enough to find the email and short enough that a link left in a browser history
# or a referrer header is already dead.
# EMAIL_LINK_MINUTES=15
# LOGIN_CHALLENGE_MINUTES=5

# Per-identity limits, on top of the per-IP limiter. Mailing a link is an action
# taken against someone else's mailbox, so what has to be bounded is requests per
# address rather than requests per requester — which the IP limiter cannot see.
# EMAIL_LINKS_PER_HOUR=5
# LOGIN_CHALLENGES_PER_HOUR=30

# How many signatures may be offered against one challenge before it is spent.
# Signatures are verified before the nonce is consumed, so a wrong one does not
# burn it and an attacker cannot grief a known address — this is what still bounds
# an open challenge as free verification work for an anonymous caller.
# LOGIN_CHALLENGE_ATTEMPTS=5

# The miner CLI's bearer token. Shorter than the browser's cookie, and unlike it,
# capped absolutely. A cookie lives in a browser its owner can inspect and clear
# and is HttpOnly; a CLI token is a file on a mining machine at mode 0600. So a
# shorter rolling window, plus a ceiling past which rolling stops — otherwise a
# nightly cron job keeps a credential alive forever. The maximum may not be below
# the window; the process refuses to start if it is.
# CLI_SESSION_DAYS=14
# CLI_SESSION_MAX_DAYS=90

# Live CLI tokens per account. A miner legitimately has several — a laptop, a
# couple of rigs, CI — and every `conjectures auth login` mints another. Reaching
# the ceiling evicts the oldest rather than refusing the newest, so a stale token
# on a decommissioned box cannot lock a miner out of the machine they are on.
# CLI_SESSIONS_PER_ACCOUNT=10

# How long a held credit stays held, and how long an account has to make a
# declared transfer. An intent holds a credit from the moment it opens, so this is
# also how long an abandoned attempt strands one.
# INTENT_MINUTES=30
# DEPOSIT_HOURS=24

# `credits[:bonus]`, comma-separated. A bonus is EXTRA CREDITS, never a discount:
# a package grants credits+bonus for credits x price. A bonus larger than its
# purchase is refused at startup as a likely typo.
# At most 10 credits may be PAID FOR per purchase; a larger package is refused at
# startup rather than hidden from the pricing page. A bonus is not bounded by that
# cap, because nobody can request it: `10:3` grants 13 credits for the price of 10.
# The default offers one credit, pay-for-5-get-1, and pay-for-10-get-3.
# CREDIT_PACKAGES=1,5:1,10:3

# A pinned USD price for display, or nothing. Converting TAO to USD needs a live
# external rate this validator does not have, so the field is null unless set —
# and setting it requires the date, because a quoted price with no date cannot be
# judged for staleness.
# CREDIT_PRICE_USD=4.50
# CREDIT_PRICE_USD_ASOF=2026-08-01

# --- Paying for credits with TMC PAY ------------------------------------------
# The second funding path: instead of transferring TAO to the treasury and waiting
# for the deposit watcher, a buyer pays a TMC PAY invoice in TAO and the processor
# settles to the treasury later, in batches, net of its commission.
#
# The price is unchanged: CREDIT_PRICE_RAO per credit, 0.5 TAO. The invoice is
# *quoted* in fiat because that is what TMC PAY accepts, and the fiat figure is
# sized from a live TAO/USD rate so the TAO the invoice locks is at least the
# credit price. Two consequences worth knowing before turning this on:
#
#   * It needs a TAO/USD rate, and needs no configuration to get one. The
#     preferred source is TMC PAY's own GET /api/v1/rates on the host below — the
#     platform quoting the rate it prices invoices with, so closest to the rate it
#     will lock. It needs no API key and none is sent. TaoMarketCap's public
#     5-minute candles are the next source and TAOSTATS_API_KEY an optional third.
#     Better than all of them, and used first once it exists, is the
#     `exchange_rate` TMC PAY reported on our own last invoice. With no source at
#     all a purchase answers 503 rather than inventing a rate.
#   * The validator pays the commission. Credits still cost 0.5 TAO each, so what
#     reaches the treasury per credit is 0.5 TAO minus TMC PAY's fee. Raise
#     CREDIT_PRICE_RAO if you need to net the full amount.
#
# Off unless all three of these are set, and a partially-configured deployment
# refuses to boot: a key with no webhook secret would create invoices it could
# never confirm, which is a buyer paying and getting nothing.
#
# TMC_PAY_API_BASE_URL=https://api.pay.taomarketcap.com
# TMC_PAY_API_KEY=<the merchant API key, shown once at merchant creation>
# TMC_PAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=<the merchant webhook signing secret>
#
# Point the merchant's webhook URL at POST /v1/webhooks/tmc-pay on this API. The
# endpoint is authenticated by an HMAC-SHA256 over the raw body keyed by the
# secret above — nothing else — so the URL may be public but the secret must not.
#
# Optional. When set, a webhook naming a different merchant is ignored instead of
# matched on invoice id: the one check that a delivery aimed at somebody else's
# integration cannot move money here.
# TMC_PAY_MERCHANT_ID=
#
# Where to send a buyer who would rather use TMC PAY's own payment page. Optional:
# every field a self-hosted page needs — deposit address, TAO amount, expiry — is
# already on the order.
# TMC_PAY_HOSTED_BASE_URL=https://pay.taomarketcap.com
#
# Log the whole request when a TMC PAY webhook fails its signature check: every
# header, the body verbatim and its digest, plus which signing scheme WOULD have
# matched the configured secret. That last line is the point — a scheme matching
# means the secret is right and the signed message is not the raw body; none
# matching means the secret is wrong. Off by default: the body carries an account
# id, so leave it on only while troubleshooting. Only the failure path logs, so a
# healthy deployment with it enabled prints nothing.
# TMC_PAY_WEBHOOK_DEBUG=true
#
# The currency the merchant is onboarded in, and its minor-unit count. Set the
# decimals to 0 for a zero-decimal currency such as JPY.
# TMC_PAY_FIAT_CURRENCY=USD
# TMC_PAY_FIAT_DECIMALS=2
#
# The quote band, in basis points. Two knobs and they are not the same thing:
#
#   MARGIN is how far ABOVE the credit price we ASK, to absorb the rate moving
#   between our estimate and TMC PAY's lock a moment later. It is spent money: the
#   overshoot is not lost, it stays in the buyer's balance as remainder_rao and
#   eventually becomes a credit.
#
#   MAX_SLIPPAGE is how far above the credit price an invoice may come back and
#   still be ACCEPTED — the acceptable overcharge. Outside it, the invoice is
#   thrown away and requoted from the rate that invoice itself reported, which is
#   exact rather than an estimate. 1% by default: loose enough that ordinary rate
#   movement does not cost a second invoice, tight enough that a stale or
#   wrong-currency rate is caught instead of charged to the buyer.
#
# One cent (one minor unit of the fiat currency) is always allowed on top of
# MAX_SLIPPAGE. That is not slippage but arithmetic: the fiat ask must round UP to
# the currency's minor unit, so the cent it rounds by is unavoidable.
#
# MAX_SLIPPAGE must be >= MARGIN or every invoice would land outside the band and
# every purchase would fail; the API refuses to start on that combination.
#
# ATTEMPTS is how many invoices one purchase may create before giving up. Two is
# enough: the first uses the estimate, the second uses the exact locked rate.
# TMC_PAY_QUOTE_MARGIN_BPS=25
# TMC_PAY_MAX_SLIPPAGE_BPS=100
# TMC_PAY_QUOTE_ATTEMPTS=2
#
# The invoice TTL (TMC PAY allows 5 to 1440), how many unpaid invoices one account
# may hold at once, and the largest single purchase. The ceilings exist because the
# endpoint's side effect is an invoice at a payment processor.
# TMC_PAY_TTL_MINUTES=30
# TMC_PAY_MAX_OPEN_ORDERS=3
# TMC_PAY_MAX_CREDITS=1000
#
# How often GET /v1/me/credits/tmc-pay/orders/{id} may re-read TMC PAY while the
# buyer watches, and how long an outbound call may take.
# TMC_PAY_POLL_SECONDS=5
# TMC_PAY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10
#
# How long a rate observed on one of our own invoices is reused to price the next
# one. Set to 0 to always ask an external feed instead. Longer means fewer outbound
# calls; the quote band makes a stale seed a wasted round trip, never a wrong price.
# TMC_PAY_RATE_TTL_SECONDS=300
#
# TaoMarketCap's public market-data host: the external TAO/USD source behind TMC
# PAY's own rate endpoint. No API key, 10 req/min, and each price is cached until
# the next 5-minute candle boundary — so a price fetched at 13:12 is reused until
# 13:15 and no further. Set to `none` to switch it off and use TaoStats alone.
# TAOMARKETCAP_API_BASE_URL=https://api.taomarketcap.com
#
# Whether a payment confirmed AFTER its invoice expired issues credits by itself.
# Off by default: TMC PAY documents `late_payment` as a manual reconciliation case,
# and this validator cannot tell from outside whether such a payment settles to the
# treasury or is returned to the sender. The order is flagged for review either way.
# TMC_PAY_CREDIT_LATE_PAYMENTS=false
#
# TMC PAY dispatches each webhook ONCE and never retries automatically, so run the
# reconciler on a schedule or a lost delivery is a buyer who paid and got nothing:
#   */2 * * * * cd /srv/conjectures && python3 scripts/reconcile_tmc_pay.py

# The terms served by GET /v1/catalog/submission-terms. The version in force when
# a submission was accepted is the version that governs it, so bump this when the
# document changes rather than editing in place silently.
# SUBMISSION_TERMS_PATH=./docs/SUBMISSION_TERMS.md
# REVIEW_POLICY_VERSION=v2
# SUBMISSION_TERMS_VERSION=v3
# SUBMISSION_TERMS_EFFECTIVE_FROM=2026-08-07

# --- Running the API with docker compose --------------------------------------
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.api.yml up -d --build
# brings up Postgres, applies migrations, then starts the API. The API port is
# bound to loopback only: TLS, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 terminate at the edge.
# API_PORT=8000
# --- Development verification worker ------------------------------------------
# docker-compose.worker.yml is development-only and uses the in-process runner.
# Production uses the host systemd service and its separate root-owned environment file in
# deploy/worker/. Never give the hostile-proof verifier container this database configuration.
# VERIFICATION_RUNNER=container
# VERIFIER_VERSION=v1
#
# Production requires the expected local image ID and refuses a tag that resolves to anything
# else. The worker then runs that image ID, not the mutable tag.
# VERIFIER_IMAGE=formal-conjectures-verifier:local
# VERIFIER_CONTAINER_DIGEST=sha256:<64-lowercase-hex-digits>
#
# Three claims of one submission is enough to establish that something is
# systematically wrong with it; a fourth costs another full task timeout and
# learns nothing. A submission at the cap waits for an operator.
# VERIFICATION_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3
# Human-readable label only; the worker appends a random per-process lease token.
# VERIFICATION_WORKER_ID=<hostname>/production-worker-1

# --- Treasury and pricing -----------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED, in every mode. The address miners send TAO to, and what one
# submission costs. `just up` refuses to start without the address, and
# docker-compose.api.yml interpolates it with no default: a treasury address
# guessed from a template is money sent to the wrong account.
#
# On Subnet 66 this is the validator's own hotkey — the same address the deposit
# watcher below is pointed at, and the two MUST match.
# PAYMENT_RECIPIENT_SS58=5Gn2SyG6PmBstAjiPD93CTuxADqYaYqf6fKeFuezKsX7Chf9
#
# 0.5 TAO, in integer rao. One submission. Quoted to a miner by the API and
# credited at by the watcher, so `just _check-watch` refuses a deployment where
# this and CREDIT_PRICE_RAO disagree.
# PAYMENT_AMOUNT_RAO=500000000

# --- Deposit watcher (python -m deposit_watcher) -------------------------------
# Reads Balances.Transfer events out of finalized blocks, records every arrival
# at the address below, and credits the account whose coldkey sent it. Every
# CREDIT_PRICE_RAO transferred buys one credit, which is one submission; the
# remainder is not discarded, it stays in the ledger balance towards the next
# credit. Started with `just up-watcher` or `just up-all`, deliberately not by
# `just up` — it reads mainnet and writes to the credit ledger.
#
# Check the configuration before switching it on. This performs the registration
# check and the timestamp-to-block bisection without crediting anything:
#   just watcher-check
#
# NONE OF THE FOUR BELOW HAS A DEFAULT. Each one decides which transfers buy
# credits, and a default about money is a mistake waiting to be inherited.

# The address to watch. On Subnet 66 it is the validator's hotkey, and it must
# equal PAYMENT_RECIPIENT_SS58 above — the API tells miners where to send and
# this is where the watcher looks.
DEPOSIT_WATCH_RECIPIENT_SS58=5Gn2SyG6PmBstAjiPD93CTuxADqYaYqf6fKeFuezKsX7Chf9

# Where that address is registered. Checked against SubtensorModule.Keys at
# startup: the watcher refuses to run if the pair does not resolve to the
# address above. That check is the only thing standing between a typo and a
# watcher that reads real blocks forever and credits nothing, quietly.
DEPOSIT_WATCH_NETUID=66
DEPOSIT_WATCH_UID=121

# The genesis timestamp. Transfers in blocks before this buy nothing.
#
# A TIMEZONE OFFSET IS MANDATORY. `2026-08-01T00:00:00` is not an instant — it is
# that wall clock in whichever zone the reader assumes, and the two readers here
# are this process and a Subtensor block header. The watcher bisects this to a
# block height once and records the result permanently, so getting it wrong is
# not undone by fixing the variable afterwards.
DEPOSIT_WATCH_FROM=2026-08-01T00:00:00Z

# One credit is one submission. Must equal PAYMENT_AMOUNT_RAO.
# CREDIT_PRICE_RAO=500000000

# `finney` is mainnet. The archive endpoint is used only by the one-off bisection
# that resolves DEPOSIT_WATCH_FROM to a block, which reads state outside a lite
# node's pruned-state window; following the finalized head does not need it.
# Defaults to the same network.
# BITTENSOR_NETWORK=finney
# BITTENSOR_ARCHIVE_NETWORK=

# How many blocks one pass reads before it sleeps. The cursor advances per block
# inside its own transaction either way, so a large backlog is resumable rather
# than one enormous transaction a restart throws away. The poll interval is
# roughly one Subtensor block.
# DEPOSIT_WATCH_BATCH_BLOCKS=200
# DEPOSIT_WATCH_POLL_SECONDS=12

# Skips the netuid/uid registration check. For a local Subtensor with no subnet
# 66 to answer about. REFUSED IN PROD.
# DEPOSIT_WATCH_VERIFY_REGISTRATION=true

# DEPOSIT_WATCHER_ID=<hostname>/<pid>

# --- Observability (Axiom) ----------------------------------------------------
# Structured events from the API and every worker: a severity, the area of the
# system that spoke, an event type, and the fields that go with it. Read by all
# four processes under the same variable names, so one setting configures the
# whole deployment and they cannot end up writing to different datasets.
#
# UNSET IS A SUPPORTED DEPLOYMENT. Unlike every other setting in this file,
# these are not fail-closed: with no token the sink is a no-op, ingestion never
# happens, and stderr logging is unchanged. Observability decides whether anyone
# is watching, not whether the validator is correct, so a typo here must cost a
# dashboard and never a verification worker.
#
# An Axiom API token, `xaat-…`. A personal token (`xapt-…`) is not an ingest
# credential. Both this and AXIOM_DATASET must be set; either alone is a
# half-finished configuration and disables ingestion.
# AXIOM_TOKEN=
# AXIOM_DATASET=conjectures-validator

# A free-form deployment tag written onto every event, so one dataset can hold
# more than one deployment and still be readable. `prod`, `staging`, a hostname.
# AXIOM_ENVIRON=default

# The API base. Set to https://api.eu.axiom.co for the EU region.
# AXIOM_URL=https://api.axiom.co

# The severity floor for the stdlib-logging bridge, which forwards the existing
# `logger.*` calls in the API and the workers as events. This can only narrow
# what reaches Axiom, never widen it: a handler never sees a record the process
# log level already filtered, so DEBUG here under `--log-level INFO` still
# forwards INFO and above.
# AXIOM_LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# How much of the HTTP surface produces a per-request event. `all` is one event
# per answered request. `failures` keeps every 4xx and 5xx and drops the
# successes, for a deployment whose public catalog traffic would otherwise
# dominate the dataset. `off` leaves the domain events and the logging bridge in
# place and stops only the request stream. Liveness, readiness and the docs are
# never reported in any mode.
# AXIOM_REQUEST_EVENTS=all
